1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,760
At a lot of large customers,

2
00:00:03,800 --> 00:00:05,790
perception is more real than reality.

3
00:00:05,830 --> 00:00:08,440
This is a podcast episode from December 2024.

4
00:00:08,480 --> 00:00:10,905
Back then Wang was Scale AI's CEO.

5
00:00:10,945 --> 00:00:13,790
Now he's Meta's chief AI officer.

6
00:00:13,830 --> 00:00:15,556
The companies everyone here has worked

7
00:00:15,596 --> 00:00:18,450
with are mostly data-driven.

8
00:00:18,490 --> 00:00:20,200
It feels like the truth makes its way.

9
00:00:20,240 --> 00:00:22,830
Everybody shares one sense of reality.

10
00:00:22,870 --> 00:00:24,602
But not at most large companies,

11
00:00:24,642 --> 00:00:27,300
nor in government, unfortunately.

12
00:00:27,340 --> 00:00:29,890
Reality is usually so ugly

13
00:00:29,930 --> 00:00:33,050
that people very rarely confront it.

14
00:00:33,090 --> 00:00:34,404
Mostly they choose

15
00:00:34,444 --> 00:00:37,070
to believe the perceptions they live in.

16
00:00:37,110 --> 00:00:43,320
So if you do enterprise sales or build enterprise products,

17
00:00:43,360 --> 00:00:44,895
your job is to improve the reality,

18
00:00:44,935 --> 00:00:47,370
just as much as to shape the perception.

19
00:00:47,410 --> 00:00:49,480
One of Palantir's superpowers:

20
00:00:49,520 --> 00:00:50,714
they shape perception

21
00:00:50,754 --> 00:00:55,750
better than most tech companies.

22
00:00:55,790 --> 00:00:57,890
Because they view themselves

23
00:00:57,930 --> 00:00:59,461
as part acting troupe,

24
00:00:59,501 --> 00:01:02,610
part software company.

25
00:01:02,650 --> 00:01:04,790
And I'm not even speaking figuratively: they literally

26
00:01:04,830 --> 00:01:08,350
gave all new hires an acting book, for a very long time.

27
00:01:08,390 --> 00:01:10,612
Ex-Palantir engineer Nabeel Qureshi wrote

28
00:01:10,652 --> 00:01:12,290
that new hires were sent a few books,

29
00:01:12,330 --> 00:01:14,860
one of them Impro, on improv theater.

30
00:01:14,900 --> 00:01:17,850
Whether they still do, he says he isn't sure.

31
00:01:17,890 --> 00:01:18,722
Fundamentally,

32
00:01:18,762 --> 00:01:20,430
you also need to build a good product.

33
00:01:20,470 --> 00:01:22,423
Sept. 23, Meta Connect:

34
00:01:22,463 --> 00:01:24,010
Wang took the stage in a T-shirt he says

35
00:01:24,050 --> 00:01:26,730
was a $30 thrift find.

36
00:01:26,770 --> 00:01:28,239
Oct. 3, Slate: Silicon Valley's most powerful men

37
00:01:28,279 --> 00:01:30,930
have discovered the appeal of looking harmless.

38
00:01:30,970 --> 00:01:34,000
Perception can amplify reality. It can't replace it.
